1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,459
On a catch-up call, I told my friend Nick Wignall how someone had trained an AI model

2
00:00:05,459 --> 00:00:07,599
to write blog posts in my style.

3
00:00:07,940 --> 00:00:10,560
It was a pure research exercise on their part.

4
00:00:11,339 --> 00:00:16,559
The idea was to train the tool on my past work, then give it the headlines and opening

5
00:00:16,559 --> 00:00:18,600
paragraphs of my 2025 posts.

6
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:21,899
Could it generate the rest of each piece in a similar fashion?

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,620
So I only compared a handful of posts from their AI versions, so it looks fine.

8
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:30,280
The A was fine enough at first glance, but pay attention just a little longer at something

9
00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:31,399
from the RFID country.

10
00:00:33,039 --> 00:00:36,359
It sounded pretty confident where I would have been doubtful and vice versa and so on.

11
00:00:36,659 --> 00:00:41,159
The creator wanted to know if such a model, once it worked properly, of course, could

12
00:00:41,159 --> 00:00:42,159
be useful to me.

13
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:46,259
I told him even if it worked perfectly, it wouldn't lie because I don't write a daily

14
00:00:46,259 --> 00:00:48,060
blog to crank out a post every day.

15
00:00:48,299 --> 00:00:51,700
If that was the point, I'd have switched to AI long ago already.

16
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:56,380
I write a daily blog to make sure I remember how to think it's a daily practice for my

17
00:00:56,380 --> 00:00:56,939
brain, a creator.

18
00:00:57,320 --> 00:00:59,460
And if not, that's fine too, attract new readers.

19
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:01,079
That's fine too.

20
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:06,859
Nick said, my story reminded him of an interview with writer and Vox, founder Ezra Klein.

21
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:11,799
Klein explained that so far AI hasn't been all that useful to him.

22
00:01:12,079 --> 00:01:16,560
He uses it for light research or to structure some data, but that's about it.

23
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:16,959
Why?

24
00:01:17,439 --> 00:01:21,200
Because the writer doing the research is what makes the writing unique.

25
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:26,480
When you're using AI as a writer, you're outsourcing the part of the work like Tend

26
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:27,840
Jovian need to do the most.

27
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:28,620
Klein believes.

28
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:35,939
This is why reading actual books in full might now be more valuable than it ever has been.

29
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:41,319
Only if you've seen every word will you discover insights and links in AI would never include

30
00:01:41,319 --> 00:01:42,879
in its average driven summary.

31
00:01:43,379 --> 00:01:47,319
Nick pointed out the same applies to a writer struggling when creating a piece, when you're

32
00:01:47,319 --> 00:01:50,200
stuck and sit there thinking, trying to come up with what's next.

33
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:55,400
That's the valuable part of writing, of attempting to use AI to remove that stuckness.

34
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:59,540
But it's basically cheating and leads to a basic unblaggian.

35
00:02:01,719 --> 00:02:06,739
But is it the idea that needs to slot into this gap or just a bad piece of filler that

36
00:02:06,739 --> 00:02:08,539
I'll make for a fragile mental bridge?

37
00:02:09,319 --> 00:02:14,840
Most readers won't dare to cross the more I think about the happier I am that AI is

38
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:17,500
transforming the world of writing in a way.

39
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:22,699
I think it'll make it even easier to stand out because the more people take shortcuts,

40
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:27,419
the less quality will remain for readers to flock to, even if the overall quantity of

41
00:02:27,419 --> 00:02:28,580
options is much larger.

42
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:34,939
The real artists, fighters, makers, they stick with the truth as old as time itself.

43
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:36,939
The suck is why we're here.

44
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:42,699
And only those who overcome it themselves will reap all the rewards of their hard labor.

